/* ============================================================================
   sample-variance.epher -- variance of a sample with the n - 1 correction

   The sample variance of the ten exam scores in `d`: the builtin variance
   divides by n (population), while the sample version divides by n - 1
   (Bessel's correction), so it is computed from the builtin as
   variance(d) * n / (n - 1). There is no separate builtin for it. With
   ten scores the population variance is 88.41 and the sample variance
   88.41 * 10 / 9 = 98.23..., which epher shows as the exact fraction
   2947/30 (its exact-fraction display: 98.2333... repeats).

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/statistics/summary/sample-variance.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\statistics\summary\sample-variance.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/statistics/summary/sample-variance.epher
   In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
   Everything also works line by line in the TUI.

   The one knob to edit is `d` below. Everything above the results section
   is machinery you can leave alone.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob -----------------------------------------------------------------
const d = {72, 85, 91, 64, 88, 79, 83, 95, 70, 86}   // exam scores, 10 students

// ---- sample variance: sum of squared deviations over n - 1 -----------------------
variance(d) * len(d) / (len(d) - 1)   // population variance rescaled by n / (n - 1)
sum((d - mean(d)) ^ 2) / (len(d) - 1) // the formula directly, same 2947/30
variance(d)                           // the population version for contrast, 88.41

/* ---- expected output ----
= {72, 85, 91, 64, 88, 79, 83, 95, 70, 86}
= 2947/30
= 2947/30
= 88.41
*/